From: HakiOS
Subject: [PATCH 0143] 0143: SWEET THERM, read-only TSENS temperatures every 5 s

What: a new bsd/dev/sweet_thermal.c (added to bsd/conf/files) with the two TSENS TM blocks of sweet's
own device tree (0x0c263000, 0x0c265000) and their SROT blocks (0x0c222000, 0x0c223000) mapped
VM_PROT_READ, and one log line every 5 s from AppleCLCD::statsReport: max CPU, A55, A76, GPU, DDR
and cpuss, in deci-C, "--" for a sensor with no valid reading. All accesses are plain 32-bit loads of
TM + 0xa0 + 4*n: bit 21 valid, bits 11:0 signed deci-C, 0xffffffff means nothing there. The 5-count
divider and the one-shot start live in the stats thread, so the first read is 5 s after the panel is
up, never during early boot.

Why: A6 raises the CPU clocks to the top of the OSM LUT, and the decision needs the temperatures of the
same run ("hottest sensor below 80 C for the whole 540 s"). Boot-arg sweettherm=0 turns the line off.
The one-shot SROT ctrl probe (bit 0 TSENS_EN, bit 21 CODE_OR_TEMP) is printed with the first reading so
the log itself says whether the codes really are deci-C (on raw ADC codes no temperature line is
printed). TM_TRDY is only logged raw: patch 0255 uses bit 3 of 0xe4 and mainline bit 0, so it is not
used as a gate; the per-sensor valid bit is.

Proof: mkpatch and tools/check-patch.py ok; applies on the series tree (0138-0142); no line over 200
characters; no store to any TSENS register (VM_PROT_READ maps, loads only). Expected lines:
  SWEET THERM: on, tsens0 TM 0c263000, tsens1 TM 0c265000, every 5 s (sweettherm=0 off)
  SWEET THERM: probe ts0 trdy ........ ctrl ........ (deci-C), ts1 trdy ........ ctrl ........ (deci-C)
  SWEET THERM: cpu 45.2 a55 44.8 big 45.2 gpu 43.1 ddr 40.9 cpuss 44.1 C
---
